# lines.view: the inverse of columns.view. The same table, the same alignment, and the
# separator turned through ninety degrees: a rule between elements, no vertical anything.
# No border, no title.
#
# The way in is a decorator over a plain markdown table, which is the whole point: where
# this hook does not run, the reader still gets a real table.
#
#   @{view:lines}
#   | | |
#   | --- | --- |
#   | Status | all green |
#   | Deploy | staging is red |
#
# The header row is MANDATORY and its cells may be empty (`| | |`): markdown needs one,
# this view never prints it.
#
# The label column spends the TONE SLOT, where columns.view puts it on the HEADER instead,
# and that is the one difference between the two files. It follows the direction each view
# rules in: this one rules ACROSS, so an element is read along its row and what names it is
# the label at the head of that row. One file still dresses every kind:
#
#   @{view:lines}                 the default below, teal
#   @{view:lines, type:warning}   yellow labels, and the message still says what it IS
#   @{view:lines, tone:dim}       grey, a look with no semantics attached
#
# The header takes the WEIGHT and no ink, the label takes both, so the two roles stay apart
# on screen: the criteria are the words across the top, the elements compared are the labels
# down the side. The tone is an INDEXED colour (see columns.view), and the label wears the
# weight BESIDE it for a reason the eye settles: an index gains no vividness from bold the
# way a base-sixteen name does, so at plain weight it reads as faded text rather than as a
# colour chosen on purpose, next to the white of the columns it heads.
#
# `@box bare` is what makes the rest work, and it is not decoration. A bare container runs
# the body machinery and draws none of the chrome, which buys three things a frameless
# template cannot have on its own: the rule is FILLED by the engine to the width of the
# widest line, so nothing here has to guess a number; a long content column WRAPS instead
# of being left to the terminal; and the blank-run collapsing drops the rule that would
# otherwise trail after the last element, which is how a divider drawn under every item
# comes out BETWEEN them.
#
# `bullet=""` carries no bullet and is not idle: a declared bullet is what puts the hanging
# boundary into the line, at `${#bullet}`, so the wrap blanks everything to its left and a
# folded content column stays in its own column instead of restarting at the margin. An
# empty one buys the indent and prints nothing.
#
# The container sizes to its CONTENT and not to the terminal, so a short table gets a short
# rule. That is the point rather than a limitation: a rule running past the body it divides
# is furniture louder than what it separates.
#
# An empty label does NOT continue the element above, the way it does in columns.view: a
# rule lands between the two. Write the long content on one row instead, and let it wrap.
#
# Two to four columns, like columns.view and for the same reason: the carrier hands this
# view whatever the table had, and a middle column it did not declare would be dropped in
# silence. They are separated by BLANKS and not by a bar, which is this view's whole
# contract, and a column no row carries takes its own blanks down with it (the hollow rule,
# src/layout/columns.ts), so a two-column table draws exactly what it always drew.
#
# @head draws once above the elements, from the table's own header line and only when that
# line says something, with a rule of its own under it: the loop's own @rule falls BETWEEN
# items and would leave the header glued to the first one. Two @head lines, because the
# header is two lines.
#
# The content column is the LAST field, emitted verbatim, so it carries whatever emphasis
# the message wrote. A quote handed to this view resolves nothing, and the raw markdown
# shows instead.
@tone teal
@fields rows label mid1 mid2 content
@box bare
@each rows cap="1/3" bullet=""
@head {{b}}${label}{{/}}  {{b}}${mid1}{{/}}  {{b}}${mid2}{{/}}  ${#bullet}{{b}}${content}{{/}}
@head @rule
{{b}}{{tone}}${label}{{/}}  ${mid1}  ${mid2}  ${#bullet}${content}
@rule
@end
@endbox
